{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'settings.define|settings.set|menu:|pauseOn|focus|visibilitychange|autoPause|pausable|_menu|voiceVolume|bus\\\\(' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/API-INDEX.md' | head -65\"","aggregated_output":"282:- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n304:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the ga [... omitted end of long line]\n312:- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n318:- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size the [... omitted end of long line]\n340:- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\n459:- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause [... omitted end of long line]\n469:- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n487:- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n488:- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / m [... omitted end of long line]\n493:- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, wa [... omitted end of long line]\n498:- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() [... omitted end of long line]\n511:- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), [... omitted end of long line]\n519:- `game.viewports` †: a split screen's viewports ({ i, x, y, w, h, cam, focus(x, y, z), follow(target, { z }): game.splitScreen), or [] on one screen\n527:- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the [... omitted end of long line]\n545:- `game.pauseReason = null`: why the game paused: 'player' | 'focus' (the page was hidden) | 'controller' (unplugged) | 'menu', or null\n546:- `game.paused = false`: true while paused (a pausable scene toggles it on 'pause'; steps still run, scenes don't update)\n554:- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n575:- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its  [... omitted end of long line]\n578:- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value [... omitted end of long line]\n637:- `saves.show(mode = 'load', done)` †: open the standard save screen over the game (E.Shell's): 'save' (to a slot) or 'load'; done(save) after a load or a save. False without a menu (a headless game,  [... omitted end of long line]\n1070:- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute a [... omitted end of long line]\n1091:- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"The title is **Orbit Invadettes**. The source research supports the plan’s core: marching rows, four erodible arch bunkers, a horizontal cannon and a finite six-wave run. I’ve pinned the engine and Hub client; the combat simulation and shared-world adapter are being built in parallel.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '565,600p' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/API-INDEX.md'; sed -n '1045,1108p' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/API-INDEX.md'; rg -n 'function Game|class Game|constructor\\\\(o|class Screen|class Settings|allowJoin\\\\(' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/my-3d2dge.js' | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '565,600p' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/API-INDEX.md'; sed -n '1045,1108p' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/API-INDEX.md'; rg -n 'function Game|class Game|constructor\\\\(o|class Screen|class Settings|allowJoin\\\\(' 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/vendor/my-3d2dge.js' | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"- `game.view`: the view the game draws in now (the chosen one, with zoom and turn: read it, change it with setView)\n- `game.views`: views this game is designed for (view cycling and the checker use it). null = every view\n- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n- `E.PLAYER_COLORS: #4a8aff, #ff4a4a, #4ad860, #ffd040` †: the players' colors: player 1 blue, 2 red, 3 green, 4 yellow (game.players)\n- `E.showError(title, e)`: show an error in the red box at the top of the page (the engine does it for errors in update and draw); a click hides it. Before play has started it shows on the boot screen instead, as a report a player can screenshot\n\n## 10b. Standard features\n\n- `settings.default(key)`: a setting's default value\n- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a valid one) and applied; it shows on the settings screen. Returns its value\n- `settings.keys(group)`: the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key\n- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)\n- `settings.spec(key)`: the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined\n- `settings.saved`: false when the last save failed (storage blocked or full: the choices last for this visit)\n- `fullscreen.enter(quiet)`: go full screen. Browsers allow it only from a tap, click or key press: call it from one (a button, the menu, a key's update step). Returns a promise of whether it worked (a refusal is kept in failed and noted on screen, unless quiet)\n- `fullscreen.exit()`: leave full screen (Escape and the phone's back gesture do it too)\n- `fullscreen.on`: true while the page is full screen\n- `fullscreen.standalone`: true when the page was opened from a home screen as an app (no browser bars: nothing to go full screen from)\n- `fullscreen.supported`: true where this page can go full screen (not an iPhone, which has Add to Home Screen instead; not a frame without permission; not headless)\n- `fullscreen.toggle()`: full screen on or off, as the player asked (the corner's button, the menu): their choice is kept in settings.fullscreen, so a game starting on a phone goes full screen by itself only while they haven't turned it off\n- `fullscreen.failed = null`: game: the game it serves; failed: why the last request was refused (a message), or null\n\n## 10c. Boot\n\n- `E.bootSplash(title = '')`: the boot screen's markup, for the very start of a page's <body>: it shows from the first paint, its bar moving while the page's scripts load (a pure CSS animation), and says so if the game's script never runs. The engine takes it over\n- `E.diagnose(game = E.current)`: the report a bug needs, at any time (and on the boot screen when a game can't start): { engine, game, time, page, browser, device, screen, canvas, gpu, sound, storage, input, scene, errors, text (all of it, one line each, for a screenshot or a bug report), short (one line) }. game: the game to report on (E.current)\n\n## 10d. Tweens and scripts\n\n- `new E.Script(game, fn, o = {}) → script` †: a script, made by game.script: done (it returned, or was cancelled), value (what it returned), cancel(); another script can yield it\n- `script.cancel()` †: stop it, and the scripts it is waiting on (its finally blocks run)\n- `script.done = false` †: it returned, or was cancelled\n- `script.keep` †: it outlives the scene\n- `script.value` †: what it returned\n- `shots.plane`: 'ground' (they fly over x and y) or 'side' (x and z)\n- `shots.sparks`: a puff of sparks where one hits a wall\n- `E.pattern`: velocity helpers for bullet patterns. Angles in radians (0 = east / right). Each returns [vx, v2] or a list of them\n- `E.pattern.aim(a, b, speed)`: aim from a to b on the ground plane (x, y); aimSide uses x, z\n- `E.pattern.aimSide(a, b, speed)`: a velocity from a toward b in a side view (x, z)\n- `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`: one velocity at an angle\n- `E.pattern.ring(n, speed, offset = 0)`: n bullets in a full circle, rotated by offset\n- `E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`: n bullets fanned across `arc` radians around `angle`\n\n## 19. Spatial hash\n\n- `new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32) → grid`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply; map: the buckets\n- `grid.add(o, x = o.x, y = o.y)`: put o in the bucket at (x, y)\n- `grid.build(list, xk = 'x', yk = 'y')`: clear, then add every live thing in list (skips o.dead); do it once a step\n- `grid.clear()`: empty it\n- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\n- `grid.query(x, y, r)`: what lies within about r of (x, y), as a list (near() calls a function instead)\n- `grid.cell`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply. cell: the bucket size in world units; map: the buckets\n\n## 20. Audio\n\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio)\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (ms: settings.audioOffset) and done is true; taps go on refining it. stop() puts the music back\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playing }\n- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing (it sounds on that step of the music clock, scheduled with the music: a rhythm game's calls and claps), pan: -1 (left) .. 1 (right), loop: true (it sounds on until stopped: an engine, rain; through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute) }. Returns its handle: { stop(fade .03), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }: a handle that does nothing when nothing plays (headless, before the first key, an unknown name)\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\n- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1\n\n## 21b. Backdrops\n\n- `new E.Backdrop(o = {}) → backdrop`: new E.Backdrop('castle-night') (a preset: E.Backdrop.presets) or new E.Backdrop({ sky: [colors top to bottom], sun, moon, stars, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, seed }] }): a parallax sky and scenery drawn behind a level. sun, moon: { x, y (0..1 of the screen), r, color }\n- `E.Backdrop.presets` †: the preset names, all ten: day, dusk, night, castle-night, city-night, desert, forest, ocean, cave, space\n- `backdrop.draw(r)` †: draw the backdrop first in draw(r), before the level; it scrolls with the camera by each layer's parallax\n- `backdrop.layers` †: its scenery layers, back to front, with their defaults filled in\n- `backdrop.sky` †: the sky's gradient colors, top to bottom\n\n## 21c. Props\n\n- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\n641:class Screen {\n2308:class Game {\n2335:  constructor(o = {}) {\n3087:class Settings {\n3724: *    numpad on the right), touch for player 1. game.allowJoin({ max, onJoin }) lets them in: Start on a free pad, or 2 (the\n3755: *  (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are String's and Array's) */\n4059:  constructor(o = {}) {\n5041:  constructor(o = {}) {\n5232:  constructor(o = {}) {\n6310:  constructor(o = {}) {\n6723:  constructor(o = {}) {\n7066:  constructor(o = {}) { Object.assign(this, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0, vz: 0, r: 5, h: 32, gravity: 700, friction: 0, bounce: 0, step: 2, onGround: true, groundZ: 0, hitWall: false, landed: fal [... omitted end of long line]\n7318:  constructor(o = {}) {\n7545:  constructor(o = {}) {\n8102:  constructor(o = {}) {\n10889:  constructor(outline, holes = []) {\n11229:  constructor(o = {}) {\n11301:  constructor(o = {}) {\n11765:  constructor(o = {}) {\n12464:  constructor(o = {}) {\n12600:  constructor(o = {}) {\n12928:  constructor(o = {}) {\n13378:  constructor(o = {}) {\n13933:  constructor(o = {}) {\n14757:  constructor(o = {}) {\n14858:  constructor(o = {}) {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/src/SIM-CONTRACT.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat 'Orbit-Invadettes-Astro-Battle-Space-Invaders-1979-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022709/src/SIM-CONTRACT.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Simulation contract\n\nES modules `content.js` and `sim.js`; no DOM/engine dependencies. Field 480×270. Cannons y246, x18..462; breach y230. Bunker center x80/186/294/400, 16×8 cells of 3px, left center-24, top202. Bunker `.mask` and `.original` are row-major numeric 0/1 arrays, `.cols=16,.rows=8,.cell=3,.x` left,.y top,.center` center x.\n\n`createWorld({seed,players=1,difficulty=1,ids})`, `stepWorld(world,commands,dt=1/120)`, `repairPreview(world,seat)`, `startEncounter(world,index)`, `setTuning(world,key,value)`, `serialize(world)`, `deserialize(data)`. Commands seat-indexed `{move:-1..1,fire,repair,targetX?}`; repair is already an edge. World has `tick,time,seed,rng,epoch,encounter,status,reason,score,reserve,extraAwarded,assisted,players,enemies,bullets,pickups,bunkers,formation,boss,saucer,events,tuning,difficulty,location,encounterTime,transitionTime,nextEncounter,killCount,stats,intro`. Encounter 0..5 formations,6 boss. status playing/transition/victory/defeat. `events` is reset each step; consumer must handle every step to retain all effects.\n\nPlayers stable `.seat,.id,.x,.y,.alive,.respawn,.protected,.charges,.score,.kills,.repairs,.recovery,.active`. `.protected` and `.respawn` seconds. `alive=false,respawn>0` pending; alive=false respawn0 spectator. `.active=false` departed seat. Bullets `.id,.x,.y,.vy,.owner` seat (friendly) or -1(enemy), `.kind='shot'/'bomb'/'heavy'`, `.r`; pickups `.id,.x,.y,.life`. Enemy `.id,.row,.col,.x,.y,.type='ordinary'/'armour'/'bombardier',.hp,.maxHp,.telegraph,.flash`; all enemy x/y absolute, synchronized from formation. Formation `.x,.y,.dir,.timer,.beat,.initial,.interval`; `.x` offset from authored start x. Boss `.x,.y,.phase=1/2,.emitters=[{x,y,hp,maxHp,open,telegraph}],.hp,.maxHp,.open,.cycle,.safeLane,.telegraph,.age,.hit`; emitter x/y absolute.\n\nRepair preview returns `{bunker:index,cells:[row-major indices],usable,reason}`; stable nearest bunker even demolished. Every cell drawn is exactly a collision cell. `TUNING` array `{key,label,min,max,step,default,description}` and `DIFFICULTIES` array contain shared slider/preset data. Export `WAVES`, `LOCATIONS`, `FIELD`, `tuningForDifficulty`, `validateContent` from content. `startEncounter` is a dev helper; caller marks assisted. `setTuning` validates bounds and marks assisted. `killPlayer`, `spawnSaucer`, `spawnEnemy`, `leaveSeat` additionally exported for dev/session actions; these never execute without explicit caller action.\n\nImplementation and verification in progress; final tests will document actual normal-run durations and found issues.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
